The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
context_clear_entry(context);
</text>
Reason:
The original teardown zeroed the entire 128‑bit context entry in a single call that uses multiple 64‑bit writes. This creates a window where the Present bit is still set while other fields are already zeroed, allowing the hardware to fetch a torn entry and cause spurious faults.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
context_clear_present(context);
__iommu_flush_cache(iommu, context, sizeof(*context));
intel_context_flush_no_pasid(info, context, did);
context_clear_entry(context);
__iommu_flush_cache(iommu, context, sizeof(*context));
</text>
Reason:
First clear only the Present bit to signal the ownership transition from hardware to software, then flush the cache and invalidate the context‑cache so the hardware no longer uses the old entry. Only after that are the remaining fields zeroed. This prevents the IOMMU from ever seeing a partially zeroed entry with the Present bit still set. Additionally, a `dma_wmb()` is added to `context_set_present()` (not shown in this patch hunk) to ensure the entry is fully initialized before the Present bit becomes visible.

